{
  "summary": "Both prior proposals correctly identify the decomposition axes but disagree on where the hard boundary lives. The real leverage point is recognizing that PipelineApp's ~40 action_* methods follow exactly two patterns that can be mechanically separated: (1) 'state-query-then-modal' actions (action_move, action_new_feature, action_unified_edit, etc.) that read current state, push a modal, and handle the callback — these can become a thin ActionDispatcher mixin or be driven by a declarative action table mapping (stage, action_name) -> handler, eliminating ~400 lines of boilerplate guard clauses; and (2) 'async-worker-launcher' actions (action_run_pipeline, action_plan_only, _run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, _auto_plan_check, etc.) that manage running/not-running flags, acquire locks, spawn @work-decorated coroutines, and call _log_line — these ~1,200 lines should move into a PipelineController that owns AgentStatus objects and exposes a simple event-emitter interface (on_log, on_done, on_rate_limit) that PipelineApp subscribes to via Textual's message system. This avoids the callback-protocol complexity the second critique raised because Textual already has a message bus — the extracted controller posts messages, PipelineApp handles them, no new protocol needed. For the _check_for_changes scalability concern, the second critique is right that filesystem watches add complexity for marginal gain at <100 features, but the current implementation has a real bug: it calls FeatureFile.list_all() on every change-detected cycle to push to the server, which re-stats ALL features across ALL boards even though only the active board changed — fixing this by caching the all-features list separately and only refreshing it on board-switch or explicit refresh would eliminate the actual scaling bottleneck without architectural changes. The modal extraction is the safest first step but should preserve co-location of the modal CSS with each modal class (as Textual supports inline CSS) rather than splitting into separate .tcss files, which would scatter related concerns."
}
